{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Habiro.MirrorPairNormalisation","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Habiro.MirrorPairNormalisation","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Group.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.GroupWithZero.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":6,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Habiro.MirrorPair.mirror_traceDiff_eq_zero_of_tau_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Habiro.MirrorPair.mirror_traceDiff_eq_zero_of_tau_eq","kind":"theorem","line":81,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Habiro/MirrorPairNormalisation.lean#L81-L86","doc":"The coincidence stated as a vanishing difference: the object whose\ndivisibility by `Φ₁ = q − 1` is being asserted. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Habiro.MirrorPair.mirror_trace_eq_of_tau_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Habiro.MirrorPair.mirror_trace_eq_of_tau_eq","kind":"theorem","line":64,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Habiro/MirrorPairNormalisation.lean#L64-L79","doc":"**The classical-point mirror coincidence.** Write a closed-form trace as\n`τ_p^Z · τ_n^N · c^n` — the shape `_build_closed_form` produces, with `c` the\ncommon value of every interface factor. Measured at `q = 1`: all three\ninterface kinds (`z`, `z − h`, `z − h/2`) equal `1/2`, and `τ_p = τ_n = 1/2`.\n\nGiven only `τ_p = τ_n` and an interface count invariant under `(Z,N) ↦ (N,Z)`\n(both verified), the mirror partners coincide. This is *why* `(q − 1)` divides\na correctly formed mirror difference — and it is a statement about the\nclassical point alone, requiring no ordering on `R`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Habiro.MirrorPair.coeffDiff_ne_zero_at_coincidence","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Habiro.MirrorPair.coeffDiff_ne_zero_at_coincidence","kind":"theorem","line":54,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Habiro/MirrorPairNormalisation.lean#L54-L62","doc":"**The failure.** Differing denominators at a coincidence point force the\ncoefficient-difference to be *nonzero* precisely where the true difference\nvanishes. This is the measured `Φ₁ = 0`: the p−n coefficient-difference\nevaluated to `6` at `u = 1`, where the trace difference is `0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Habiro.MirrorPair.coeffDiff_eq_zero_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Habiro.MirrorPair.coeffDiff_eq_zero_iff","kind":"theorem","line":47,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Habiro/MirrorPairNormalisation.lean#L47-L52","doc":"Consequently, at a coincidence point the coefficient-difference vanishes\n**iff** the denominators agree there or the common trace value is zero. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Habiro.MirrorPair.coeffDiff_at_coincidence","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Habiro.MirrorPair.coeffDiff_at_coincidence","kind":"theorem","line":40,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Habiro/MirrorPairNormalisation.lean#L40-L45","doc":"**The broken regime, exactly.** At any point where the two traces agree,\nthe coefficient-difference collapses to the *denominator gap* times the common\ntrace value. It retains no information about the traces at all. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Habiro.MirrorPair.coeffDiff_of_common_denom","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Habiro.MirrorPair.coeffDiff_of_common_denom","kind":"theorem","line":31,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Habiro/MirrorPairNormalisation.lean#L31-L38","doc":"**The valid regime.** With a shared denominator, differencing the\ndenominator-cleared numerators is differencing the traces, scaled by that\ncommon denominator. Cyclotomic multiplicities of the result therefore differ\nfrom those of `t_X − t_Y` only by the multiplicities of `d`. This is why the\nconvention-C computation gave the right answer despite taking the shortcut. ","signatureChunk":0}]}